PACKS=[]

PACKS.append(dict(title='Notifications, attention and useful reminders',context='learning-cognition',code='TEC.04',
a='**Source A: simulated laboratory experiment.** Sixty consenting adult volunteers are allocated by chance to a silent-phone condition or a condition with neutral phone notifications, 30 per condition. They complete the same ten-minute attention task without touching the phone. The figure shows the mean percentage of target trials on which participants make an error. Error bars are one standard deviation, not confidence intervals. Individual results are not shown.',
b='**Source B: separate simulated repeated-measures experiment.** Forty adult volunteers complete equivalent attention tasks with notifications blocked and enabled, in a randomly assigned, counterbalanced order. The mean error percentages are 5.0% with notifications blocked and 7.0% with notifications enabled. A supplied paired analysis estimates an enabled-minus-blocked mean difference of 2.0 percentage points, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.6 to 3.4 percentage points. Researchers allow a break between tasks. They do not assess course learning or later examination performance.',
c='**Source C: simulated qualitative study.** Eight first-year university volunteers are interviewed about managing study messages. The interviewer helped design a reminder application and initially expects all notifications to be distracting. One fictional participant says, “I lose my place when a message arrives, even if I ignore it.” Another says, “The reminder helps me return after a break instead of forgetting the session.” The interviewer selects only extracts mentioning interruption for the first report and does not ask participants to comment on that interpretation. These invented excerpts are not quotations from a published study.',
labels=['Silent phone','Notifications'],values=[4,7],errors=[2,4],maximum=15,ylabel='Mean target-trial errors (%)',
graph=['The bars summarise group means; they do not show how every person performed. A higher notification-group mean does not imply that each participant in that group made more errors than every silent-phone participant. The supplied standard deviations indicate dispersion, and individual results could overlap substantially.',
'This limits a claim that notifications impair attention uniformly across people. Random allocation strengthens comparison of the two assigned conditions, but does not replace the missing individual distribution or prove a universal response. It would also be incorrect to treat overlap of standard-deviation bars as a statistical test of the mean difference.'],
data=['The enabled condition has a mean error rate 2.0 percentage points higher than the blocked condition, from 7.0% versus 5.0%. This is a difference in percentages, not a two-percent relative increase. The reported confidence interval remains above zero, so the supplied analysis supports a positive average difference under its assumptions.',
'A paired design compares each person across the two conditions and reduces the influence of stable individual differences. Counterbalancing helps separate condition from task order, but does not guarantee removal of every practice or carry-over effect. The data support an average attention-task cost of enabled notifications in this sample.',
'The conclusion should remain about the measured laboratory errors. Neither these means nor their confidence interval establishes that every individual is harmed, or that blocking notifications improves retained course learning. Those broader outcomes were not measured.'],
qualPrompt='Discuss how the researcher could avoid bias when interpreting Source C.',
qual=['The interviewer’s involvement in the reminder application creates an expectation that can shape selection and interpretation. Reporting only interruption extracts excludes the participant who describes a helpful reminder, making the initial account narrower than the supplied material. This is a concrete selection decision rather than a reason to discard all interviews.',
'The researcher should record their position reflexively, retain an audit trail of how extracts and interpretations were chosen, and deliberately examine material that challenges the initial expectation. Another researcher can review interpretations and disagreements against the transcripts, rather than merely endorse the first account.',
'Participants could be invited to comment on whether the interpretation fairly represents their accounts, with confidentiality protected. Such feedback is not a vote that establishes one correct meaning. Together these procedures make competing readings more visible and reduce one-sided reporting; they cannot make the researcher entirely free from perspective.'],
claim='Blocking notifications always improves student learning.',
synthesis=['The claim is stronger than the supplied evidence. Sources A and B concern a short laboratory attention task, whereas “student learning” can include understanding, retention and successful return to study. A defensible judgement must separate a plausible attentional mechanism from proof of all those outcomes.',
'Source A gives a higher mean target-error rate under neutral notifications. Chance allocation and the same task strengthen a condition comparison, but group summaries do not establish a uniform effect. Standard-deviation bars describe variability rather than the uncertainty of a population mean, and the volunteer task setting restricts the reach of the finding.',
'Source B adds a within-person comparison and a positive mean difference with a confidence interval above zero. This converges with the average attention cost in Source A while retaining possible task-order and carry-over limitations. It still supplies no delayed learning measure, so the statistical support cannot be transferred automatically to examination performance.',
'Source C offers relevant meanings that the laboratory outcomes omit. One participant reports interruption; another describes a reminder that supports returning to study. The biased selection of extracts weakens the initial qualitative report, but the contrasting account nevertheless challenges the word “always”. Eight university volunteers cannot represent every learner or type of notification.',
'Psychological knowledge provides a mechanism and an alternative. Limited attentional resources and switching between goals can disrupt an ongoing task. A deliberately timed external cue can also support remembering an intended action. Notification purpose, timing and the learner’s current goal therefore matter, rather than technology having one fixed effect.',
'Overall, selectively blocking irrelevant notifications during demanding tasks is consistent with these simulated attention findings. Universal improvement in learning is not established. A stronger investigation would compare notification types and measure later learning as well as immediate errors, while representing the experiences of learners with different study needs.'],
checks=[assertion('Notification mean difference',7-5,2,0,'Percentage-point difference, not relative percentage.'),assertion('Lower mean-difference interval above zero',float(.6>0),1,0,'The supplied paired interval supports a positive average difference; no raw-data p-value is invented.')],sources=[STOTHART]))

PACKS.append(dict(title='Time online and different forms of support',context='health-wellbeing',code='TEC.01',
a='**Source A: simulated cross-sectional survey.** Adult volunteers report their daily time on social applications and complete a general well-being scale scored from 0 to 40, with higher scores indicating greater reported well-being. The figure shows mean scores for three time categories: under 30 minutes, 30–120 minutes and over 120 minutes. The sample was recruited through an online notice. No experimental assignment, baseline measure or adjustment for background differences is used.',
b='**Source B: separate simulated randomised study.** One hundred consenting adults are allocated equally to a voluntary reduction in social-application use or their usual use for eight weeks. Well-being is measured on a general 0–40 scale, with higher scores indicating greater reported well-being. Twelve people from the reduction group and four from the usual-use group do not provide the final well-being questionnaire. Among those with final responses, mean improvements are 3.0 and 2.0 scale points respectively. The supplied between-group estimate for improvement is 1.0 point, with a 95% confidence interval from -0.3 to 2.3 points. The analysis excludes the missing final responses. This is not a diagnostic or treatment study.',
c='**Source C: simulated interview study.** Ten adults from an online mutual-interest group describe how participation fits into everyday routines. They are recruited purposefully from long-term active members, all with reliable internet access. A fictional participant says, “I found people who share my hobby when there was no local group.” Another says, “I keep scrolling after the conversation is finished.” The report describes the group, recruitment and interview setting in detail. The excerpts are invented for this stimulus, not taken from real participants.',
labels=['Under 30 min','30–120 min','Over 120 min'],values=[27,29,25],errors=None,maximum=40,ylabel='Mean reported well-being (0–40)',
graph=['The three bars compare different self-selected time-use groups at one occasion. They cannot establish that changing an individual’s time online would cause their well-being to move to the mean of another group. People may differ in existing well-being, reasons for using an application or offline circumstances.',
'This limits a causal reading of the lower mean in the highest-use category. Well-being might influence use, a third factor might influence both, or a relationship may depend on the type of use. The descriptive pattern remains legitimate, but the cross-sectional grouping does not isolate a time-use effect.'],
data=['Among completers, the reduction group improves by one scale point more on average than the usual-use group. The reported 95% confidence interval ranges from -0.3 to 2.3, so it includes zero and differences in either direction. This does not provide clear support for a positive average difference under the supplied analysis; it also does not prove that the true difference is exactly zero.',
'The original equal allocation is a design strength, but final responses are missing from 12 reduction participants and 4 usual-use participants. The completer analysis may therefore compare groups affected differently by attrition. If non-completers experienced the procedure differently, randomisation alone does not protect this final comparison.',
'A proportionate conclusion is that the observed completer contrast is one point on a 0–40 scale and remains uncertain, with an important missing-data limitation. No threshold for a practically important difference is supplied. The evidence does not justify a universal well-being benefit, nor the opposite universal claim that reduction cannot help anyone.'],
qualPrompt='To what extent are the findings of Source C transferable to other populations or contexts?',
qual=['The detailed description of the group and interview setting helps a reader judge whether another setting shares the relevant conditions. Access to a valued interest community may be relevant to adults with few local opportunities, while continued scrolling may arise in quite different forms of application use.',
'The sample deliberately consists of long-term active members with reliable access. People who left the group, dislike online interaction or lack reliable connectivity are not represented. Their experiences may differ in ways central to the research aim, so the findings cannot be treated as a population estimate of benefit or harm.',
'Transferability is therefore conditional on contextual similarity and the usefulness of the described processes, not guaranteed by the number ten. Additional accounts from former members and less well-connected settings could broaden understanding. A qualitative account can offer informative insights without statistically representing every person who uses social applications.'],
claim='Reducing time on social applications is necessarily better for well-being.',
synthesis=['The claim treats time online as one uniform exposure and improvement in well-being as a necessary result. The three sources instead concern different populations, methods and kinds of participation. Their combined value lies in identifying conditional possibilities rather than supplying a universal instruction.',
'Source A shows the highest reported well-being in the middle time category, not a simple monotonic decline as time increases. Because the survey is cross-sectional and self-selected, the pattern cannot reveal the direction of influence. Existing circumstances or the purposes of use might explain group differences, and the category means conceal varied individual experiences.',
'Source B strengthens causal reasoning through random allocation. However, its one-point completer advantage has an interval that includes zero, and the unequal loss of final responses threatens the comparison. These results are compatible with uncertainty about an average effect; they neither establish a necessary benefit nor demonstrate an exact absence of benefit.',
'Source C adds meanings that a total-time measure misses. A shared-interest group can provide valued contact while habitual scrolling can continue after its social purpose ends. Long-term active members with reliable access are a restricted perspective, but their contrasting accounts explain why equal time totals need not involve equal psychological processes.',
'Knowledge of social identity and the need for relatedness can explain the value of access to a meaningful group. Learning and reinforcement processes can also sustain repeated checking beyond a person’s goal. These possibilities make the kind of interaction, autonomy over use and offline opportunities relevant to the outcome; neither mechanism implies that every user experiences the same effect.',
'The necessary-benefit claim is not supported. The sources justify studying differentiated patterns of use and individual contexts, with stronger handling of missing outcomes and longitudinal measurement. Any judgement should stay about the measured, non-diagnostic well-being outcomes and avoid converting this constructed evidence into a clinical recommendation.'],
checks=[assertion('Completer mean improvement contrast',3-2,1,0,'Scale points, not percentage change.'),assertion('Reduction completers',50-12,38,0,'Allocated equally; twelve missing.'),assertion('Usual-use completers',50-4,46,0,'Allocated equally; four missing.'),assertion('Confidence interval contains zero',float(-.3<=0<=2.3),1,0,'Absence of a clear positive difference is not proof of no effect.')],sources=[ORBEN]))

PACKS.append(dict(title='External memory and the outcome being tested',context='learning-cognition',code='TEC.04',
a='**Source A: simulated task comparison.** Consenting adults answer factual questions under two conditions. In one condition they may consult saved digital notes; in the other they must answer without notes. The figure shows mean correct answers out of 20: 19 with notes and 14 without notes. The outcome is successful answering under the stated resource conditions. The figure does not show a later unaided memory test.',
b='**Source B: separate simulated experiment.** Eighty consenting adults are randomly assigned to learn factual material while expecting future access to saved notes or expecting no future access. Both groups later take the same unaided factual-recall test and a test of where the material was stored. Mean factual scores out of 20 are 12 with expected access and 15 without expected access; the supplied access-minus-no-access difference is -3, with a 95% confidence interval of -4.6 to -1.4. Mean location scores out of 6 are 5 and 3; the supplied difference is 2, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.8 to 3.2. These are two different outcomes, not parts of a single memory total.',
c='**Source C: simulated workplace interviews.** Ten adult volunteers describe using digital checklists in an unfamiliar administrative task. A fictional participant says, “I remember where the rule is and check it when an exception comes up.” Another says, “If the system is unavailable, I have to stop.” The researcher labels every reference to checking a rule as “dependency”, without looking for cases where checking prevents an error. The invented extracts are practice material, not published quotations.',
labels=['Notes available','No notes'],values=[19,14],errors=None,maximum=20,ylabel='Mean correct answers (out of 20)',
graph=['The measured outcome is answering with different resources available, not memory under equivalent testing conditions. A person allowed to consult notes can answer successfully without internally recalling the fact. The higher bar therefore cannot show that note access improved that person’s unaided memory.',
'The comparison may still be useful for understanding successful task performance with resources. Its limitation is the mismatch between that outcome and a claim about internal retention. A later common unaided test would be needed to address retention directly; relabelling the existing vertical axis as “memory” would not solve the problem.'],
data=['The expected-access group recalls three fewer factual items on average, but locates two more stored items correctly. Both supplied difference intervals exclude zero in opposite directions: the factual interval is negative and the location interval is positive. The pattern therefore supports a shift across the two measured outcomes rather than a single uniform improvement or impairment.',
'Random assignment strengthens an inference about expecting future access under the experimental procedure. A plausible interpretation is that expectations change what participants prioritise during encoding: the content itself or a route to recovering it. That mechanism remains an interpretation, rather than a directly observed mental process.',
'The conclusion should distinguish factual recall from location memory and remain within the supplied task and sample. Adding the two scores would mix different scales and constructs. The data do not demonstrate permanent memory damage, nor do they establish what happens when notes are actually available during a real work task.'],
qualPrompt='Evaluate how the researcher could reduce interpretative bias in Source C.',
qual=['The single “dependency” label builds a negative interpretation into the coding choice. The participant who remembers where a rule is may be describing an effective division of memory work, not merely an inability to function. Ignoring possible error prevention makes the coding less open to contrasting meanings in the accounts.',
'A reflexive account should state the researcher’s assumptions about unaided memory and document how the label was developed. Deliberately examine counterexamples and consider alternative interpretations, such as checking as a safety strategy. A second researcher can review the fit between extracts and interpretations and record disagreements rather than suppress them.',
'Participants may be invited to comment on whether the account captures their intended meaning, while the researcher retains responsibility for analysis. These procedures increase transparency and consideration of alternatives; they do not prove that one label is the only possible reading or remove the influence of the research setting.'],
claim='Using digital external memory weakens human memory and therefore reduces task performance.',
synthesis=['The claim joins two propositions that need to be evaluated separately: a reduction in internal memory and a resulting reduction in task performance. “Memory” is not one outcome in these sources, and resource-assisted performance is not identical to unaided recall. That distinction is central to a justified judgement.',
'Source A gives higher factual-task performance when notes are available. Because the resource conditions differ, the comparison cannot establish an improvement in internal retention. It does, however, challenge the idea that reliance on an external store necessarily lowers successful performance under conditions where that store remains available.',
'Source B provides a more controlled test of expectations about future access. Lower factual recall is accompanied by better memory for location, with the supplied intervals supporting opposite average differences on the two outcomes. This is consistent with a change in what information is prioritised, rather than evidence that every memory process is weakened.',
'Source C illustrates a possible functional benefit and a vulnerability. Locating a rule can help a worker check an exception, while system failure can leave that worker unable to continue. The one-sided “dependency” coding weakens the report, and the small volunteer sample limits the range of circumstances represented. The accounts nevertheless expose conditions omitted by a laboratory recall score.',
'Knowledge of cognitive offloading or transactive memory offers an explanatory framework: people can distribute information between internal representations and reliable external resources. This may reduce the need to retain some details while freeing effort for other task demands. Dependence on availability, inaccurate stored information or inadequate checking can still create costs.',
'The global impairment claim is not supported. These constructed sources suggest a trade-off between different memory functions and resource-supported performance. A stronger investigation would measure unaided retention, accurate use of external information and performance during resource failure, rather than infer all three from one score.'],
checks=[assertion('Factual recall difference',12-15,-3,0,'Different outcome from location memory.'),assertion('Location memory difference',5-3,2,0,'Do not add scores across constructs.'),assertion('Resource-assisted task contrast',19-14,5,0,'Successful task answering is not an unaided retention test.')],sources=[SPARROW]))

PACKS.append(dict(title='Ranking feedback and relationship meanings',context='human-relationships',code='TEC.03',
a='**Source A: simulated platform report.** A platform reports mean feelings-of-connection ratings on a 0–8 scale for two kinds of group: a moderated discussion group and a group with a public popularity ranking. The figure gives means of 6.2 and 5.0. It does not supply the number of respondents, variability, recruitment procedure or whether people belong to both kinds of group. The report is descriptive, not an experiment.',
b='**Source B: separate simulated survey.** A volunteer sample of 120 adults reports exposure to public popularity rankings and completes a social-comparison-worry scale. The supplied correlation is r = 0.32, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.15 to 0.47. The report gives r-squared as approximately 0.10. Measures are collected at the same occasion; no assignment to ranking exposure occurs and background differences are not controlled. Higher scale scores indicate more comparison worry, not a diagnosis.',
c='**Source C: simulated qualitative study.** An insider researcher interviews nine consenting members of one adult discussion community. The community has recently rejected public popularity rankings. One fictional participant says, “The ranking made quiet contributions seem unimportant.” Another says, “It helped me find people who posted useful answers.” The researcher was a prominent supporter of removing rankings and knows each participant. The first draft quotes only people who supported removal. These are invented accounts for practice.',
labels=['Moderated group','Public ranking'],values=[6.2,5],errors=None,maximum=8,ylabel='Mean connection rating (0–8)',
graph=['The figure supplies no sample sizes or variability, so the stability and spread behind the two means cannot be assessed. A mean based on a few highly similar respondents and a mean based on a large varied group can look identical as bars. The 1.2-point difference alone does not establish a precise population difference.',
'This restricts how confidently the platform can generalise the comparison. Sampling and uncertainty information would be needed alongside the means. It would also be inappropriate to use the missing uncertainty information to declare that the groups are equal; the evidence is incomplete rather than a demonstration of no difference.'],
data=['The positive correlation indicates that greater ranking exposure tends to accompany more comparison worry in this volunteer sample. The interval from 0.15 to 0.47 remains positive but leaves uncertainty about the magnitude. An r of 0.32 is an imperfect relationship, not a prediction that every highly exposed person reports high worry.',
'The supplied r-squared of about 0.10 describes the share of variation associated with this simple linear relationship in the sample. It does not mean that rankings cause ten percent of a person’s worry, or that removing them would produce a ten-percent improvement. Exposure and worry were measured together without random assignment or adjustment.',
'The findings support an association that merits explanation. Existing comparison concerns could influence platform choice, or other characteristics could affect both reports. A conclusion about a causal effect of the ranking system requires a stronger design and appropriate measures, rather than only a positive correlation and an interval above zero.'],
qualPrompt='Explain how a more credible account of members’ experiences could be developed from Source C.',
qual=['The researcher’s community role gives useful contextual knowledge but also creates a risk that disagreement is muted or excluded. The supplied opposing account is relevant to the aim and should not disappear because it challenges the decision the researcher supported. A credible report needs to show how interpretation fits the range of material.',
'Retain an audit trail of recruitment, interview questions, extract selection and interpretative changes. Seek and examine contradictory accounts, and ask a researcher who was not involved in the ranking decision to scrutinise the connections between transcripts and claims. Explicitly reflect on how insider relationships may shape interviews.',
'Provide sufficient contextual description and, with appropriate confidentiality, invite participants to comment on the fairness of the account. Such feedback does not require everyone to agree or make their accounts representative of all platforms. Credibility improves through transparent, critically tested interpretation rather than by collecting only statements that confirm the preferred story.'],
claim='Algorithmic popularity feedback inevitably weakens human relationships.',
synthesis=['“Inevitably” requires a universal harmful outcome that none of these sources can establish. They use different meanings of relational quality: a connection rating, comparison worry and participants’ explanations of community experience. A careful synthesis should preserve those differences while examining possible shared processes.',
'Source A shows a higher connection mean in moderated groups, but supplies neither sample sizes nor variation. Recruitment and overlap between groups are also unknown. The difference is a reason to ask further questions, not evidence that public ranking caused weaker relationships for everyone exposed to it.',
'Source B supplies a positive, imperfect association between exposure and comparison worry. Its positive interval and approximate ten-percent shared variation quantify an association under the stated analysis; they do not identify direction or remove background differences. Worry may affect platform choice, and a worry score is not itself a direct measure of all relationship outcomes.',
'Source C reveals contrasting meanings: rankings can devalue quiet contributions for one member while helping another locate useful contributors. Insider familiarity can aid contextual understanding, but the researcher’s advocacy and selective quotations weaken the initial account. The excluded perspective is especially important to judging a universal claim.',
'Psychological knowledge suggests more than one mechanism. Social comparison and reinforced pursuit of visible approval may alter participation, whereas social identity and shared goals may make an online community supportive. The design of feedback, group norms and the meaning attached to a ranking can therefore matter as much as the presence of an algorithmic signal.',
'The sources support investigating conditional risks, not inevitable relationship damage. Stronger evidence would distinguish platform features and relationship outcomes, examine change over time or an ethically implemented intervention, and represent contrasting user experiences. A qualified judgement is more defensible than either universal harm or universal benefit.'],
checks=[assertion('Connection mean difference',6.2-5,1.2,1e-12,'No unknown sample size or SD is invented.'),assertion('Squared correlation',.32**2,.1024,1e-12,'Approximately0.10 shared variation; not causal attribution.')],sources=[ORBEN]))

PACKS.append(dict(title='Adaptive practice across adult age groups',context='human-development',code='TEC.02',kind='lines',
a='**Source A: simulated before–after study.** Twenty consenting adults aged 18–25 and twenty aged 55–65 use the same adaptive puzzle application for four weeks. Both age groups take the same form of an application-related accuracy task before and after practice. Mean accuracies are 55% then 73% for the younger group, and 62% then 76% for the older group. The figure connects the group means. Every participant receives the application; there is no group that repeats the tests without the application. The figure does not show individual trajectories or uncertainty intervals.',
b='**Source B: separate simulated randomised experiment.** Ninety consenting adult volunteers aged 18–25 are assigned equally to adaptive practice or fixed-difficulty practice on the same platform for six weeks. Practice time is equal. The groups improve by means of 12 and 8 percentage points on trained item types. A supplied analysis estimates an adaptive-minus-fixed mean improvement difference of 4 percentage points, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.5 to 7.5. No measure of an untrained everyday task or later follow-up is collected.',
c='**Source C: simulated qualitative study.** Twelve adults aged 55–65 who completed an application course are interviewed about learning. All describe themselves as confident device users. People who discontinued the course are not invited. One fictional participant says, “I developed a strategy for the puzzles.” Another says, “I enjoyed the routine, but I do not know whether it changed anything outside the application.” The report describes access to devices, prior experience and the training setting. These are invented accounts, not published participant quotations.',
labels=['Age 18–25','Age 55–65'],values=[55,62],after=[73,76],errors=None,maximum=100,ylabel='Mean task accuracy (%)',
graph=['Both groups receive the application and repeat the same test form. Without a group that repeats the tests without the application, an improvement cannot be isolated from familiarity with that test or another change occurring during the four weeks. Connecting the means does not make the change a controlled application effect.',
'The figure is still an accurate description of the supplied before–after group means. Its limitation concerns causal interpretation, not whether the displayed values rise. A suitable comparison condition and an outcome less dependent on repeated exposure would help distinguish application-specific improvement from these alternatives.'],
data=['The adaptive group improves by four percentage points more on average than the fixed-difficulty group, from gains of 12 versus 8. The supplied confidence interval, 0.5 to 7.5, lies above zero and supports a positive average difference under the stated analysis, while allowing uncertainty about the size of that difference.',
'Random assignment and equal practice time strengthen the comparison of adaptive and fixed-difficulty practice in these adult volunteers. The contrast is not application use versus no application: both groups practise on the platform. The relevant inference is therefore about the adaptive feature under this procedure.',
'The measured outcome concerns trained item types at six weeks. There is no supplied evidence of improved everyday functioning, lasting change after practice stops or effects in other ages. A proportionate conclusion supports the specific average training advantage while reserving those broader developmental claims for further evidence.'],
qualPrompt='Assess the transferability of the account in Source C to other learners or settings.',
qual=['Descriptions of the devices, prior experience and training setting help readers judge relevance to another group of learners. The accounts can illuminate strategy development and enjoyment among confident device users who complete this course; they are not numerical estimates of how commonly those experiences occur.',
'The recruitment excludes people who discontinued and those without confidence or access. These are particularly relevant omissions if the aim concerns the role of technology across adult development. A setting with limited connectivity, different support or less familiarity could generate experiences absent from these twelve accounts.',
'Transfer is therefore conditional. Including varied users and accounts from people who stopped, while explaining their contexts, could broaden understanding. It would still be inappropriate to treat qualitative transferability as a guarantee that all learners will improve, or to infer a lasting performance effect from a participant’s enjoyment of the routine.'],
claim='Adaptive applications produce lasting improvements in general cognitive development at every age.',
synthesis=['The claim combines breadth across ages, general cognitive improvement and durability. The supplied studies measure much narrower outcomes and use different comparison conditions. They allow a reasoned discussion of training possibilities, but do not establish the entire claim.',
'Source A shows improved means after four weeks in both adult age groups. The older group has a higher final mean but a different starting point; neither final ranking alone describes which group changed most. More importantly, every participant uses the application and repeats the same test form, so practice effects or other changes remain alternatives to an isolated application effect.',
'Source B supplies a stronger comparison of adaptive with fixed-difficulty practice. Its positive four-point mean improvement contrast and confidence interval above zero support an average advantage for the adaptive feature under the procedure. Both conditions involve platform practice, however, and the measured item types are trained ones. This is not evidence of broad transfer to everyday cognition.',
'Source C contributes accounts of strategy and engagement among older completers. The participant who is unsure about effects outside the application makes a distinction that the claim overlooks. The confident-device-user sample excludes discontinuers and less well-supported learners, so its contextual richness should not be mistaken for universal age coverage or an estimate of training effectiveness.',
'Relevant psychological knowledge distinguishes learning a task from demonstrating change in a broad cognitive capacity. Feedback, manageable challenge and reinforcement can support engagement and task-specific strategy acquisition. Neuroplasticity is an explanatory possibility for learning, but these sources contain no neural measure and cannot establish a particular brain change simply from better puzzle scores.',
'The general and lasting claim is not supported. The constructed evidence is consistent with a limited adaptive-practice advantage and useful experiences for some learners. Untrained transfer tasks, delayed follow-up, suitable controls and varied ages and access conditions are needed before extending that conclusion to durable general development at every age.'],
checks=[assertion('Younger mean change',73-55,18,0,'Change differs from final ranking.'),assertion('Older mean change',76-62,14,0,'No individual trajectory is inferred.'),assertion('Adaptive minus fixed gain',12-8,4,0,'Percentage points on trained item types.'),assertion('Positive supplied gain interval',float(.5>0),1,0,'No inference about unmeasured durability or transfer.')],sources=[]))

def trajectory(p):
    body=text(300,26,'Simulated before–after group means',22,'middle')+text(80,65,'Mean task accuracy (%)',21)
    x0,x1,y0,y1=80,570,310,95
    for value in [0,20,40,60,80,100]:
        y=y0-value*(y0-y1)/100
        body+=line(x0,y,x1,y,'#ddd2c4',1)+text(67,y+6,str(value),20,'end')
    body+=line(x0,y0,x1,y0)+line(x0,y0,x0,y1)
    for i,(label,before,after) in enumerate(zip(p['labels'],p['values'],p['after'])):
        color=['#7b202d','#896300'][i];xs=[190,460];ys=[y0-before*(y0-y1)/100,y0-after*(y0-y1)/100]
        body+=line(xs[0],ys[0],xs[1],ys[1],color,3,'8 5' if i else None)
        for x,y in zip(xs,ys):body+=f'<circle cx="{x}" cy="{y}" r="6" fill="{color}"/>'
        lx=[95,340][i];body+=line(lx,383,lx+32,383,color,3,'8 5' if i else None)+text(lx+40,390,label,20)
    body+=text(190,347,'Before',22,'middle')+text(460,347,'After',22,'middle')
    body+=text(300,426,'Constructed group means; not individual trajectories.',18,'middle')
    return svg(body,600,445,'Line chart of simulated group mean accuracy before and after practice')

def build():
    questions=[];records=[]
    for pack_index,p in enumerate(PACKS):
        chart=trajectory(p) if p.get('kind')=='lines' else bars(p['labels'],p['values'],p['maximum'],title='Simulated data — '+p['title'],ylabel=p['ylabel'],errors=p.get('errors'))
        elements=list(ET.fromstring(chart));graph_checks=[]
        if p.get('kind')=='lines':
            points=[e for e in elements if e.tag.rsplit('}',1)[-1]=='circle']
            expected=[v for pair in zip(p['values'],p['after']) for v in pair]
            assert len(points)==len(expected)
            for i,(point,value) in enumerate(zip(points,expected)):
                graph_checks.append(assertion(f'Source A trajectory point {i+1} vertical coordinate',float(point.attrib['cy']),310-value*215/100,1e-9,'Actual SVG coordinate checked against the supplied percentage on the zero-to100 axis.'))
        else:
            rectangles=[e for e in elements if e.tag.rsplit('}',1)[-1]=='rect' and e.attrib.get('fill')=='#f5db7e']
            assert len(rectangles)==len(p['values'])
            for i,(bar,value) in enumerate(zip(rectangles,p['values'])):
                graph_checks.append(assertion(f'Source A bar {i+1} top coordinate',float(bar.attrib['y']),325-value*210/p['maximum'],.01,'Actual plotted top must encode the supplied Source A value on the labelled zero-baseline axis.'))
                graph_checks.append(assertion(f'Source A bar {i+1} height',float(bar.attrib['height']),value*210/p['maximum'],.01,'Actual plotted height must match Source A value and full scale, not a different numerical outcome.'))
        all_sources='\n\n'.join([p['a'],p['b'],p['c']])
        items=[('p3-graph',p['a'],'Explain one issue that limits interpretation of the information in Source A.',p['graph'],chart,'medium'),('p3-data',p['b'],'Analyse the findings in Source B and state a conclusion supported by those findings.',p['data'],None,'hard'),('p3-qualitative',p['c'],p['qualPrompt'],p['qual'],None,'medium'),('p3-synthesis',all_sources,'Using at least three supplied sources and your own knowledge of the role of technology, discuss the validity of this claim: **'+p['claim']+'**',p['synthesis'],chart,'hard')]
        for j,(section,source,prompt,steps,diagram,difficulty) in enumerate(items):
            number=pack_index*4+j+1
            stem=f'**Technology stimulus pack {pack_index+1}: {p["title"]}.** All data, studies and interview excerpts in this pack are simulated for practice.\n\n'+source+'\n\n'+prompt
            concepts=[['measurement','causality'],['measurement','causality'],['perspective','bias','responsibility'],['measurement','causality','perspective','change']][j]
            q=question('tec',number,p['title']+' — '+['Graph interpretation','Data and conclusion','Qualitative research','Source synthesis'][j],p['code'],section,stem,steps,difficulty=difficulty,context=p['context'],concepts=concepts,diagram=diagram,caption='Original chart of Source A’s constructed data. '+('Error bars show one standard deviation.' if p.get('errors') else 'No uncertainty bars are supplied.'))
            questions.append(q)
            checks=graph_checks if j==0 else p.get('checks',[]) if j==1 else graph_checks+p.get('checks',[]) if j==3 else []
            records.append(evidence(q,'This item assesses '+section+' using its specifically supplied source, without converting a population average into a universal individual or causal claim. '+steps[-1],sources=[GUIDE]+p['sources'],checks=checks,figure={'labels':p['labels'],'values':p['values'],'after':p.get('after'),'errors':p.get('errors'),'maximum':p['maximum'],'baseline':0,'meaning':'The coordinates linearly encode the simulated values. Lines connect group means, not individual trajectories. Any dispersion bars are explicitly SD and are not significance tests.'} if diagram else None,alternatives=['Credit another source-specific limitation or proportionate interpretation if scientifically justified.']))
    return questions,records

if __name__=='__main__':
    questions,records=build()
    assert len(questions)==20, 'Complete all five distinct stimulus packs.'
    binding=write_collection('tec',questions)
    dump(DOCS/'technology-author.json',{'status':'author-checks-passed; independent review required','reviewer':'root technology author','questions':len(questions),'files':[binding],'records':records,'openFindings':[]})
